* ============================================================================
* rel_name_ambiguity_regression.dts
*
* AIF-120 I1.3a. THE NAME COLLISION IS ALREADY PREVENTED, AND THE LEDGER
* THAT MEASURES IT READS ZERO FOR A GOOD REASON.
*
* WHAT THIS SPEC ASSERTED FIRST, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG
*   Cut 1 built its fixture with two USE ... ALIAS NAMDUP calls and expected
*   two open areas to share one logical name. The second was REFUSED:
*
*     USE: refused -- alias 'NAMDUP' is already held by area 1.
*
*   The guard exists. cmd_use.cpp:944-972 resolves the name BEFORE touching
*   the target area: an EXPLICIT alias already held is refused outright, and a
*   name DERIVED from the file stem that is already held is auto-renamed to
*   <stem>2 and ANNOUNCED. Its own comment names this exact case -- "the
*   ordinary AGAIN case, and also two same-named files from different
*   directories."
*
*   So R112 sec 3's measurement ("USE ... ALIAS sets the logical name with NO
*   uniqueness check at all") is STALE. It was true at 8aca9ef1b and stopped
*   being true when USE_AGAIN's alias arm landed on 2026-08-12. R112 sec 6a
*   scheduled the within-workspace PREVENT half as work still to do; it was
*   already shipped, by a different lane, and nobody noticed.
*
* WHAT FOLLOWS FROM THAT, and it is the point of this spec
*   Two open areas in one workspace CANNOT share a logical name. So the
*   ambiguity ledger (WORKSPACE REGISTRY, `name ambiguity : N resolution(s)`)
*   is STRUCTURALLY ZERO today -- not because nothing was tried, but because
*   the condition it counts is unreachable until two workspaces can be open at
*   once and cross-workspace names are permitted to repeat.
*
*   R112 sec 6a predicted this in as many words: the cross-workspace half
*   "would record zero for the wrong reason, and a zero that means 'nothing
*   was tested' is exactly the false green trap-4 is about." That is what
*   happened, to the instrument built under that very ruling. Recorded here
*   rather than left for someone to rediscover.
*
* WHAT THE MARKERS NOW PROVE -- the two-directories case, which nothing else
* covers. USE_AGAIN's alias arms cover the SAME file opened twice (UA_T6/T7/T8)
* and the explicit-alias refusal. Neither covers R112's actual measurement:
* twelve basenames present in all three of dbf/x64, dbf/x32 and dbf/vfp. This
* opens two of them and proves the rename fires.
*
*   N_T1 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. The first instance is parked on record 2 while
*   the second sits at record 1, so the two areas are told apart BY FIELD
*   VALUE despite holding copies of the same data. `SELECT students` must
*   reach the FIRST holder (Martin, record 2). If the auto-rename were removed
*   both areas would answer to `students` and first-wins would still send
*   SELECT to area 0 -- so N_T1 alone does not prove the rename. N_T2 does.
*
*   N_T2 IS THE ARM THAT DIES IF THE RENAME DIES. `SELECT students2` must
*   reach the SECOND instance (Taylor, record 1). With no rename there is no
*   such name, SELECT fails, the current area stays area 0 -- which is parked
*   on Martin -- and this marker READS RED rather than vanishing. That is
*   deliberate: an errored marker prints nothing rather than going red
*   (USE_AGAIN's finding), so the assertion is arranged to be answerable from
*   an area that is always open.
*
* READ-ONLY. No fixture is created and nothing is erased. It opens two tables
* from the MCC corpus and closes them. The earlier cut built three throwaway
* tables in SANDBOX to manufacture a collision the engine will not allow.
*
*   Standalone:  DO rel_name_ambiguity_regression
*   Regression:  REGRESSION RUN NAME_AMBIG
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-BEGIN"

REL CLEAR ALL
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

* --- first instance: the x64 STUDENTS, named by its stem -------------------
SET PATH DBF DBF/x64
SELECT 0
USE students
GO 2
? "N_G0_first_instance_parked_on_record_2:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Martin")

* --- second instance: a DIFFERENT FILE with the SAME STEM ------------------
* R112 sec 3's twelve-basename collision, built from the corpus rather than
* manufactured. The block below MUST contain the rename announcement.
SET PATH DBF DBF/x32
FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-ANNOUNCE-BEGIN"
USE students IN 1
FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-ANNOUNCE-END"

* --- the two names resolve to the two areas --------------------------------
SELECT students
? "N_T1_first_name_still_reaches_the_first_holder:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Martin")
SELECT students2
? "N_T2_renamed_second_instance_is_addressable:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "Taylor")

* --- the instrument, read ---------------------------------------------------
* MUST print `name ambiguity : 0 resolution(s)`. Zero is the CORRECT answer
* and it is printed rather than omitted so that "no collision occurred" and
* "nothing is instrumented" cannot look the same (AIF-118). It becomes
* non-zero on the day two workspaces can hold the same name -- which is
* AIF-078 stage 4, and this line is the tripwire for it.
FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-LEDGER-BEGIN"
WORKSPACE REGISTRY
FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-LEDGER-END"

* --- teardown --------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

FORMULA "NAME-AMBIGUITY-END"
